model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Revoking a native grant records its pool cleanup in the tools host's auth store, then marks the grant revoked and deletes its access and refresh tokens, and only then asks the pool. The device's next access check, refresh, tool call and model request are refused whether or not the pool answers, and a revoke whose cleanup can't be recorded changes nothing. The pool's part, the computer row and any model key at Home, is tried once in the revoking request; while the pool or Home does not answer, the revoke still succeeds and a server-only endpoint retries it every minute from Cloud's cron and self-host's background loop, least recently tried first, for 30 days. The retry removes a device only once its grant is revoked or gone, and drops a row it can't read. Connect revokes a native device by its computer at the tools host before it asks the pool, so a pool or Home that does not answer can't keep the device working; revoking it again records and tries its cleanup again. A device's own revoke needs only its bearer's token, grant and consent rows, not the pool or its tools membership. The same every-minute pass checks live tools-only native grants against the pool's existing check, since none of their own checks asks it: in grant id order from a saved cursor, at most 50 a pass, and a grant again only after a full cycle and at least 10 minutes since the cycle began. When the pool answers that the device's person revoked it, the pass revokes the grant and its tokens as any revoke does; any other refusal, such as access removal, keeps the grant. While the pool does not answer, the pass changes nothing, logs one line and leaves its cursor for the next pass. The decide endpoint's cleanup of a lost approval race uses the same revoke. A failed grant record or a failed approval check cleans up at the pool inline and is not retried; no grant or token exists for that device."
    }
  }
}
